@title Database for Member Profiles
@subtitle Decision due 2026-11-04
@author Accounts team, Lattice Fitness

The member-profile service will hold 9 million profiles of about 2 KB each. At peak it sees 3500 reads per second and 220 writes per second. Most reads are key lookups, with a few admin searches by email and gym.

# Comparison

table = Option,Query flexibility,Scaling model,Ops effort,Multi-region; PostgreSQL (RDS),high,vertical plus read replicas,medium,read replicas only; DynamoDB,low,automatic,low,global tables; MongoDB Atlas,medium,sharding,medium,global clusters

# Cost at scale

data cost = level,PostgreSQL,DynamoDB,MongoDB Atlas; Today,780,540,910; 3x traffic,1640,1420,1870; 10x traffic,4300,4650,5200
chart bar cost x=level y=PostgreSQL,DynamoDB,MongoDB Atlas unit=$ labels title="Estimated monthly cost by traffic level"

# Pros and cons

### PostgreSQL (RDS)

- Pro: flexible queries for the admin searches.
- Con: scales vertically, with read replicas for reads.
- Con: multi-region means read replicas only.

### DynamoDB

- Pro: automatic scaling and low ops effort.
- Pro: global tables for multi-region.
- Con: key-based access, so admin searches need extra indexes.

### MongoDB Atlas

- Pro: flexible queries, and global clusters.
- Con: scaling means sharding, which we would have to plan.
- Con: the highest estimated cost at every traffic level.

# Recommendation

! PostgreSQL, because admin search needs flexible queries, and its cost stays within 220 dollars a month of DynamoDB at our realistic 3x horizon.

!! Write volume above 2000 per second would force us to revisit sharding.
